The name in Greek means "happy" or "rich". The harbour was one of the main [[ emporia | emporia-ancient-greece ]] on the [[ Black Sea ]]: cereals, fish and slaves went out to [[ Greece ]], and Attic goods came in for [[ Scythia ]].[[ ^1 ]] !!Pontic Olbia!! (Ancient Greek: [[ Ὀλβία | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Ὀλβία ]] [[ Ποντική | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Ποντικός ]]; Ukrainian: Ольвія, [[ romanized | romanization-of-ukrainian ]]: //Olviia//), or simply !!Olbia!!, was a Hellenic city founded in the 7th century BC by [[ colonists | colonies-in-antiquity#greek-colonies ]] from [[ Miletus ]], on the shore of the [[ Southern | southern-bug ]] [[ Bug estuary ]] (//Hypanis// or // [[ Ὕπανις | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Ὕπανις ]]//) in [[ Ukraine ]], near the village of [[ Parutyne ]].

What is left is an [[ archaeological site ]] of an [[ ancient Greek | ancient-greece ]] city, protected as the [[ National Historic and Archaeological Reserve | list-of-historic-reserves-in-ukraine ]]. The reserve is a research and science institute of the [[ National Academy of Sciences of Ukraine ]]; in 1938–1993 it was a department of the [[ NASU Institute of Archaeology ]].

== Layout

The colony covers fifty hectares, and its fortifications trace an isosceles triangle about a mile long and half a mile wide.[[ ^2 ]] Around it lay several villages (modern Victorovka and Dneprovskoe) which may also have been settled by Greeks.[[ ^2 ]]

The town had two levels. The lower town, now largely submerged by the Bug river, was given over chiefly to the dockyards and the houses of artisans; the upper town was the main residential quarter, laid out in square blocks around the [[ agora ]]; and a defensive stone wall with towers ringed the whole.[[ ^3 ]] The upper town is also where the first settlement stood, in the [[ archaic period | archaic-greece ]].[[ ^2 ]] From the 6th century there is evidence that the town was laid out on a grid plan – one of the first after the town of [[ Smyrna ]].[[ ^2 ]]

Later the city gained an [[ acropolis ]] and, from the 6th century BCE, a religious sanctuary.[[ ^2 ]] In the early 5th century a temple to [[ Apollo Delphinios | apollo ]] was built as well.[[ ^2 ]]

== History



== Archaic and Classical periods

The colony was highly important commercially, and it endured for a millennium. The earliest evidence of Greeks here, though, is not at Olbia itself but on [[ Berezan Island ]], where pottery has been found dating from the late 7th century.[[ ^4 ]] The name in Greek means "happy" or "rich". It is possible that Berezan had been the site of an earlier native settlement, and it may even have been a peninsula rather than an island in antiquity.[[ ^4 ]] The town there is now thought to have survived until the 5th century BCE, when it was possibly absorbed into the growing Olbian settlement on the mainland.[[ ^4 ]]

During the 5th century BCE [[ Herodotus ]] visited, and his is still our best description from antiquity of the city and its inhabitants.[[ ^5 ]]

In the same century the city was casting its own bronze money, in two forms: circular tokens with [[ Gorgon ]] heads, and coins in the shape of leaping [[ dolphins ]].[[ ^6 ]] Both are unusual beside the struck, round coins common in the [[ Greek world | ancient-greece ]]. This form of money is said to have originated as sacrificial tokens used in the Temple of [[ Apollo Delphinios ]].

[[ M. L. West | martin-litchfield-west ]] speculated that early [[ Greek religion | ancient-greek-religion ]], and the [[ Orphic Mysteries | orphicism ]] in particular, was heavily influenced by Central Asian [[ shamanistic | shamanism ]] practices. A significant amount of Orphic graffiti unearthed in Olbia seems to testify that the colony was one major point of contact.[[ ^7 ]]

== Hellenistic and Roman periods

In the 4th century BCE the town adopted a democratic constitution, and a treaty came to regulate its relations with [[ Miletus ]][[ ^9 ]] – one that allowed both states to coordinate their operations against [[ Alexander the Great ]]'s general [[ Zopyrion ]] in the 4th century BCE. By the end of the 3rd century the town had declined economically[[ ^1 ]] and accepted the overlordship of King [[ Skilurus ]] of [[ Scythia ]]. It flourished under [[ Mithridates Eupator ]]. Then the [[ Getae ]] under [[ Burebista ]] sacked it, a catastrophe which brought Olbia's economic prominence to an abrupt end.

Two-thirds of its settled area was lost. The Romans restored Olbia, but on a small scale and probably with a largely barbarian population. [[ Dio of Prusa ]] visited the town and described it in his //Borysthenic Discourse// – the town was often called [[ Borysthenes ]], after the river.

Incorporated into the Roman province of Lower [[ Moesia ]], the settlement was eventually abandoned in the 4th century CE, a century in which it was burnt at least twice in the course of the [[ Gothic Wars ]].

== Excavation

The site, designated an archaeological reservation, lies near the village of [[ Parutyne ]] in [[ Mykolaiv Raion ]]. Before 1902 it belonged to the Counts Musin-Pushkins, who did not allow any excavations on their estate. Professional excavation came under [[ Boris Farmakovsky ]], from 1901 to 1915 and again from 1924 to 1926. Because the site was never reoccupied, the finds – inscriptions and sculpture in particular – proved rich. Many of the more notable finds from the period can be seen in the [[ Hermitage Museum ]] in [[ Saint Petersburg ]], Russia.

The work is not finished, and there is pressure on archaeologists to explore the site, which the [[ Black Sea ]] is eroding. In 2016 the Polish Archaeological Mission "Olbia" of the [[ National Museum in Warsaw ]], headed by Alfred Twardecki, started excavations at Olbia.

Among the notable finds from the town are an archaic Greek house in a good state of preservation, from the area of the later acropolis, and a private letter written on a lead tablet around 500 BCE, complaining about an attempt to claim a slave.[[ ^4 ]]

